{"builder":"Make one pull request that adds a --tsv flag to the CLI and documents it.\n\n1. Read bin/csv2json.js, src/parse.js, test/csv.test.js and README.md. src/parse.js already exports parse(text, sep = ','), so the parser can take a tab separator as it is.\n2. In bin/csv2json.js, check whether process.argv contains --tsv. If it does, call parse with '\\t' as the separator. If it does not, keep the current comma behaviour exactly as it is. Stdin reading and output format (JSON.stringify of the array plus a trailing newline) must not change.\n3. Add a test to test/csv.test.js that runs node bin/csv2json.js --tsv with input 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' and asserts the output equals what node bin/csv2json.js prints for 'a,b\\n1,2\\n' (that is, '[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]\\n'). Keep the existing tests passing.\n4. Update README.md so it documents --tsv: show a usage line such as `node bin/csv2json.js --tsv < table.tsv` and say that it reads tab-separated input.\n5. Run npm test and make sure every test passes. Also run a quick manual check: printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv should print the same thing as printf 'a,b\\n1,2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js.\n6. Open the pull request with a description that refers to https://github.com/o/csv2json/issues/5.","verifier":"Run every check at the pull request's head, from the repository root. Each check stands for one expect line of https://github.com/o/csv2json/issues/5, in the same order.\n\nCheck 1. Expect line: \"node bin/csv2json.js --tsv reads tab-separated input on stdin and prints the same JSON array that node bin/csv2json.js prints for the same table as CSV\"\nRun at least these table pairs, giving the TSV form to --tsv and the CSV form to the plain command, and compare stdout:\n- printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv  vs  printf 'a,b\\n1,2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js\n- printf 'name\\tage\\tcity\\nAnn\\t30\\tOslo\\nBo\\t41\\tRome\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv  vs  printf 'name,age,city\\nAnn,30,Oslo\\nBo,41,Rome\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js\nPASS: for each pair, the --tsv command's output parses as a JSON array that is deep-equal to the JSON array the plain command prints for the CSV form (for example, compare with node -e and JSON.parse, or diff the outputs). FAIL: for any pair, --tsv errors, prints something that is not a JSON array, or prints an array that differs from the CSV command's array.\n\nCheck 2. Expect line: \"README.md documents --tsv\"\nRead README.md at the head (for example grep -n -- '--tsv' README.md, then read the text around it). PASS: README.md mentions the --tsv flag and says what it does (reads tab-separated input). FAIL: README.md does not mention --tsv, or mentions it without saying what it does.\n\nAdvisory (not PASS/FAIL conditions):\n- Run npm test and report whether it passes, and whether a test covers --tsv.\n- Check that plain CSV output with no flag is the same as before (printf 'a,b\\n1,2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js prints [{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}] and a newline).\n- Note whether the byte-for-byte stdout of the two commands in Check 1 matches, along with exit status 0."}